Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:02.389 align:start position:0%
 
friends and Greece are introducing  mandatory vaccinations for health care  workers in a bid to tackle increasing  coronavirus infection rates on Monday  Greg prime minister kiriakos mitsotakis  ordered all staff in hospitals and  retirement homes to book an appointment  before mid-august saying the country is  not going to close down because of the  attitude of some people  and in France president Emmanuel macron  has announced a September deadline for  medical workers to be inoculated he says  the option of a compulsory vaccination  for every citizen is also on the table  we must move towards the vaccination of  everyone in France because it's the only  way to return to normal life  initially for nursing and non-nursing  staff in retirement homes clinics  hospitals establishments for people with  disabilities for volunteers who work in  contact with elderly or fragile people  including at home  vaccination will be made compulsory  without delay obligatory  foreign  and for more now I am joined by DW  correspondent Lisa Lewis let's bring her  in now from Paris Lisa tell us what's  the reaction among citizens  well people were largely in favor of  such a compulsory vaccination for health  workers as you said it that concerns  doctors nurses and also non-nursing  staff at uh you know at hospitals at  retirement homes and I've talked to some  of those working there who told me you  know I'm hesitating to get vaccinated  I'm not outright against it but I want  to see the possible side effects on  other people first now these people will  have to get vaccinated until  mid-september if they don't they will  lose their jobs and also won't receive  they won't receive any money anymore so  the effect of that that will be that you  know many people will probably get  vaccinated but some also will leave  their jobs and you know go and work in  another sectors that they've told me  quite dramatic um tell us the compulsory  job for healthcare workers it's not the  only measure that macron announced  absolutely he also announced that all of  us would need the so-called past sanitar  that is a certificate that shows that  either you receive the two Jabs at least  you know two weeks ago or you recently  recovered from covid or you have a  negative PCR test and you will need that  pass from next week on to get access to  all cultural venues where there are at  least 50 people and from early August on  to get access to cafes restaurants  including their Terraces to a shopping  centers to trains to planes to long  distance buses basically all those that  are not vaccinated will basically be  excluded from social life it really it  will be a personal lockdown for  non-vaccinated people especially as the  PCI tests that are at the moment for  free here in France will then we will  have to pay for them from October on the  government is really cracking down on  those that are refusing to get the job  because the government says you know  this is not the choice between you know  vaccination and normal life it's the  choice between vaccination and normal  life and another lockdown especially as  the very contagious you know Delta  variant here of covid-19 is on the rise  in France Lisa Lewis with the view from  France thank you so much Lisa  and France has labeled neighboring Spain  as a coronavirus Red Zone as two regions  there reintroduce measures to Halt  Rising infection numbers residents in  Catalonia will be limited to Gatherings  of no more than 10 people and public  activities must end at half past  midnight  in Valencia a nighttime curfew is being  implemented between the hours of 1am and  6 A.M  infections among unvaccinated young  people are contributing to the surge  let's bring a DW correspondent John  Philip Schultz who is standing by with  the view from Seville in Spain so tell  us a little bit more about the  skyrocketing infection rates there  well it's complicated as always with a  pandemic but experts point out two main  factors first of all Delta of course  contrary to many European countries  Spain decided to lift all restrictions  on travelers from the UK in May and  experts agree this contributed to the  rapid spread of the Delta variant here  the second reason is Big parties some  weeks ago the feeling was in Spain that  the pandemic is basically over and this  went hand in hand with the spirit of the  upcoming summer season so a lot of young  people went on with their traditional  huge end of the school year parties and  they turned out into huge super  spreading events in one case on the  island of Mallorca 600 students got  infected in one evening  we we just heard John Philip um that  France and Greece you know in response  to Rising case numbers there that they  um are now making the job mandatory for  for some groups how about in Spain is  something similar being considered  well some Spanish regions that have a  lot of political uh autonomy talked  about such ideas in the past but there  hasn't been a big discussion on uh the  national political level yet I think  this has to do a lot with the fact that  the general willingness to get  vaccinated is very high here in Spain  service so that it's among the highest  in in all of Europe uh almost half of  the entire Spanish population is already  fully uh vaccinated and among the most  vulnerable groups including doctors and  nurses the rate is above 90 percent in  many cases I think this has a lot to do  with the fact that especially at the  beginning of the pandemic Spain was  extremely affected by the virus we saw  pictures of overwhelmed hospitals and  this has had a long lasting  psychological effect on the Spaniel  yeah I'm Phillip Schultz bringing us up  to date with the view from Seville  thanks so much  thank you  well the horrors witnessed by health  workers during the peak of Spain's  coronavirus crisis have left deep mental  scars many are now in therapy While  others have left their jobs completely  we spoke with some of those living with  the traumatic aftermath  the atmosphere at the corona world of  sendal Hospital in Madrid seems almost  relaxed right now  many birds are empty at the moment  for internist Clara Hernandez Blanco the  days have again become a bit more  bearable  she and her colleagues live through the  worst of the pandemic it has left them  deeply traumatized  I fell into a big hole I cried in the  corner I couldn't take hearing anything  more about covet victims because in the  end they were also my victims in a way  then last February and March 400 people  would die every day that was devastating  many were already desensitized at that  point but of each of these 400 I can  give you at least two or three names and  the names of the children for me these  are not numbers  it also weighed on her that for months  she could not see her daughters to avoid  possible infections Hernandez Blanco is  now in therapy  it helped me to understand that no one  outside our field can really understand  what we have lived through in here it  wasn't told and we also don't want to  talk about it  many colleagues in the hospitals in  Madrid share that opinion the experience  was too traumatic  first studies show that at least 40  percent of medical professionals need a  psychological assessment and some  experts even think that there's already  another pandemic that will soon spread  worldwide the one that affects Mental  Health  Hospital 500 out of 8500 professionals  require extra help for instance in the  psychiatric ward which has set up  special support groups  we already put preventive groups in  motion before it all escalated  these are groups where people can talk  about their experience  so you have doctors nurses help us and  one of our psychologists who share what  they're worried about  between the frustration of not knowing  how to use their expertise like under  normal conditions and not knowing what  you deal with a huge scar has developed  and it's putting a strain on medics  for Laura Sans this strain forced her to  leave her job the Intensive Care medic  contracted covid-19 and then went back  to work in the ICU despite suffering  from long covet  we're not superheroes when you work in  the Intensive Care Unit you're used to  24 48 or 72-hour shifts almost without a  break without help you are strong you  can do that and all of a sudden you  can't anymore then it's time to look for  help  I went to work crying then at work I saw  how the nurses would cry in the kitchen  or where the patients were the situation  was desperate  I found them like this I cried with them  and then I cried on my way back home in  the car  at some point I said to myself it cannot  go on like this  position she doesn't regret  many of the affected Medics are now  processing what they have witnessed they  hope the long queues that Spain's  vaccination centers will keep the beds  at the ICU free so they will really have  time to heal

